  "body": "When a text model generates words, it scores every possible next token with raw numbers. A math function turns these scores into percentages that add up to one hundred. This whole list of percentages is called the full softmax distribution.\n\nTop-k filtering changes this list by keeping only the top few choices and throwing away the rest. Look at the bar chart to see how discarding low choices increases the percentages of the remaining items.\n\nBecause the remaining percentages must still add up to one hundred, the model divides them by a new total. This renormalization step means that the reported probability of every kept token goes up.",
  "questions": [
    "What happens to the tokens that are dropped during top-k filtering?",
    "Why must the remaining token probabilities add up to one hundred?",
    "How does top-p filtering differ from top-k filtering?"
  ],
