"body": "Imagine a language model that assigns raw scores to five possible next words. After turning scores into percentages, the probabilities look like the left bars in the chart.\n\n[[visual:1]]\n\nThe chart’s right bars show what happens when we keep only the three most likely words (top‑3) and set the others to zero. The remaining three probabilities are then scaled so they still sum to 100 %.\n\nBecause the chosen token is often one of the top three, its reported chance rises after filtering. In this example, token 1’s chance grows from about 29 % to 38 %.\n\nThe effect is simply a redistribution of probability mass: the filtered‑out tokens lose all chance, and the kept tokens share the freed mass proportionally.\n\nThus, top‑k filtering does change the reported probability of the token you finally select, making it higher if the token survives the filter.",
