  "body": "Yes, top-k filtering changes the reported probability of the chosen token because it drops the lowest scores and rescales the rest. When generating text, a model computes raw scores called logits for every vocabulary item and applies the softmax function to turn them into a probability distribution.\n\n[[visual:1]]\n\nLooking at the grouped bar chart, the full softmax bars show the original probabilities before truncation. Once we keep only the top-$k$ tokens, we discard the tail and divide each remaining probability by the sum of the kept masses.\n\nThis renormalization forces the surviving tokens to sum to one, inflating their individual values. Consequently, the reported probability of a chosen token is higher after filtering than it was in the initial full distribution.",
